    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 251802\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1102 AM PDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert this week.\n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region today and \n  Wednesday, with gusty winds being the greatest threat.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Monday.\n\nA ridge of high pressure generally situated over the Four Corners \nregion will continue to gradually shift westward over the next \ncouple of days, which will bring increasing heights to the Desert \nSouthwest. As a result, temperatures will continue to climb and will \npeak on Wednesday and Thursday. As a result, there remains an \nExtreme Heat Warning in effect through Friday for the Mojave Desert. \nWhile afternoon high temperatures have been this hot (or hotter) \nearlier in the summer without heat headlines, it is important to \nnote that there is historically a resurgence of heat-related impacts \nat the end of the season. Surrounding climates are cooling off, \nresulting in tourists having decreased heat acclimation. \nAdditionally, locals have been fighting this heat all summer, so \nbodies are exhausted, and more likely to experience impacts. To \nfurther put this heat into context, please see the CLIMATE section \nat the bottom. \n\nThe Four Corners high is allowing for a southeasterly flow aloft, \nwhich has brought PWATs near 1.00 inch to the Mojave Desert and near \n0.75 inches to the southern Great Basin. There is not a ton of \ninstability across the region, but afternoon daytime heating will \nallow for some spotty convection across the forecast area today (250-\n500 J/kg of CAPE) and a weak vort max will allow for more widespread \nconvection on Wednesday. Forecast HRRR soundings show a pronounced \ninverted V due to the dry air that remains at the surface, with 1200-\n1800 J/kg of DCAPE. For this reason, the primary concerns from \nshowers and thunderstorms that form today and Wednesday will be \nerratic gusty winds from their direction. With stronger cells that \nform, cannot rule out the potential for isolated flash flooding. \nMoisture will gradually decrease as the work week progresses due to \nthe aforementioned area of high pressure shifting westward and \nbringing a dry southwesterly flow into the region. \n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nTypical light wind patterns are expected through the period as \neast winds become southwest tonight with speeds under 8KT. Light \nand variable winds Wednesday morning will become east again \nWednesday afternoon. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are \npossible in the terrain of the Southern Great Basin and western \nArizona this afternoon then again Wednesday afternoon, but in \ngeneral it will remain dry and impacts from precipitation are not \nexpected in or around the terminals. Temperatures will exceed 100F\nthrough 04Z this evening then again 17Z-04Z tomorrow. VFR skies \nprevailing. \n  \nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Typical daytime and \nnighttime wind patterns with winds generally under 10KT are expected \ntoday and tonight. KDAG will briefly gust out of the west to around \n20KT this evening like they typically do, but gusts should end by \n09Z tonight. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are possible in the \nterrain of the Southern Great Basin and western Arizona this \nafternoon, diminishing after sunset. VFR skies prevailing through \nthe period.\n&&\n\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           TUE, AUG 25 WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     111(2011)   109(2001)*  110(2017)*  110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2020)   103(2019)*  103(2019)*  103(2017)\nNeedles       119(1924)   122(1924)   120(1891)   116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(1985)   111(2011)   111(2017)   111(2017)\nKingman       106(2020)   106(2020)*  107(2017)*  107(2009)\nDesert Rock   107(1985)   106(2001)*  107(2017)   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  123(2011)   122(2017)*  124(1924)   120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      TUE, AUG 25 WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     87(2020)    88(2020)*   87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        65(1988)    66(1988)    67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       92(2020)*   93(2011)    93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       81(2003)*   82(1981)*   81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       78(2011)    79(1945)    79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1998)*   78(1985)*   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  98(1991)*   98(1991)*   95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
